  "problem": "Consider the set $A,$ defined as\n\n$$\nA={{a}_{n}\\,:\\,{a}_{n+2}={a}_{n+1}+2{a}_{n}\\,,\\,{a}_{1}=2,\\,{a}_{2}=4,\\,n\\in N}.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following sets is equivalent to $A?$",
  "worked_solution": "The elements of $A$ are all the terms of the *recursive* sequence ${a}_{n},$ given by\n\n$$\n{a}_{n+2}={a}_{n+1}+2{a}_{n},\\,{a}_{1}=2,\\,{a}_{2}=4,\\,n\\in N.\n$$\n\nLet's write down the first few terms of our sequence:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} & =2 \\\\ {a}_{2} & =4 \\\\ {a}_{3} & ={a}_{2}+2{a}_{1} \\\\  & =4+2(2) \\\\  & =8 \\\\ {a}_{4} & ={a}_{3}+2{a}_{2} \\\\  & =8+2(4) \\\\  & =16 \\\\ {a}_{5} & ={a}_{4}+2{a}_{3} \\\\  & =16+2(8) \\\\  & =32 \\\\  & \u22ee\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\nA={2,4,8,16,32,\u2026}.\n$$",
